Cholecystitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Continuous RUQ pain >6 hours, Positive Murphy's sign, Fever, Thickened gallbladder wall on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-009
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting severe right upper abdominal pain for the past 8 hours, accompanied by fever and repeated vomiting.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated continuous, severe right upper quadrant pain lasting beyond the typical duration of biliary colic, accompanied by systemic signs of inflammation (fever). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively evaluate for a localized surgical abdomen, administer parenteral analgesia/antiemetics, and determine the necessity for acute hospital admission prior to the next in-hours consulting period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've had gallbladder pain before, but it usually goes away after a few hours. This time it's been constant all night and the pain shoots right through to my shoulder blade."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports severe, continuous right upper quadrant (RUQ) abdominal pain lasting greater than 8 hours, initially starting after a fatty meal. Pain radiates directly to the right scapula. Accompanied by high fever, chills, severe nausea, and recurrent vomiting. Reports systemic malaise and anorexia. Denies dark urine, pale stools, or visible yellowing of the eyes. Known history of asymptomatic gallstones.
Functional Status: Bedbound, writhing in pain, unable to find a comfortable position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Unable to maintain hydration due to vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.4 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, diaphoretic, and in obvious distress due to pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized right upper quadrant guarding and rigidity. Exquisitely positive Murphy's sign characterized by a sharp inspiratory arrest upon deep subcostal palpation during inspiration. No clinical jaundice or scleral icterus observed. Bowel sounds are present but hypoactive. Clinical presentation confirms continuous RUQ pain >6 hours, positive Murphy's sign, and fever; a thickened gallbladder wall is anticipated on formal ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty antacid packets on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol and ibuprofen 4 hours ago with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Cholecystitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Biliary Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The duration of pain exceeding 6 hours, accompanied by fever and a definitively positive Murphy's sign indicating established localized inflammation rather than transient spasm.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain localized strictly to the RUQ with radiation to the scapula and a positive Murphy's sign, rather than epigastric burning relieved by food or antacids.

Right Lower Lobe Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of cough, clear lung fields on auscultation, and the presence of severe, localized abdominal peritonism.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with prolonged, severe right upper quadrant pain, fever, and a classic inspiratory arrest on palpation (Murphy's sign). This constellation of findings definitively shifts the clinical picture from simple, self-limiting biliary colic to acute cholecystitis, representing a localized intra-abdominal infection and inflammatory process. An urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to confirm the diagnosis, provide immediate parenteral symptom relief, and arrange prompt surgical evaluation. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to prolonged severe pain, worsening dehydration, and a significant risk of gallbladder perforation or empyema.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg and IM Ondansetron 4mg. Facilitated direct referral to the Emergency Department for surgical assessment, IV antibiotics, and definitive imaging (ultrasound).
Positive Behavioral Change: Take slow, shallow breaths when the pain spikes rather than deep, gasping breaths. This mechanically prevents your diaphragm from pushing down onto the inflamed gallbladder, reducing the sharp, catching pain you feel during inspiration.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Post-surgical or post-discharge GP review within 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to ED is recommended. If patient initially declines, they must proceed to ED immediately if pain becomes generalized across the entire abdomen, if vomiting contains blood, or if they develop clinical jaundice (yellow eyes).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Final diagnosis requires an ultrasound at the hospital to visualize the gallbladder wall.
Clinical Course: The condition requires hospital admission for intravenous antibiotics and potentially surgical removal of the gallbladder during this admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the Emergency Department. If your pain suddenly spreads across your entire stomach and becomes much worse, or if you feel extremely dizzy, call an ambulance immediately, as this could indicate the gallbladder has burst.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A gallstone has become permanently wedged in the neck of your gallbladder. Unlike previous attacks where the stone fell back, this prolonged blockage has caused the gallbladder wall to become acutely inflamed and infected. The injections will dull the pain temporarily for your trip to the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Do not consume any food or drink, not even water. The surgeons will require your stomach to be completely empty so they can safely administer anesthesia if emergency surgery is required to remove the infected organ.

